{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) achieved 0.591 acc / 0.573 F1, a substantial ~10.5-point gain over the singleprofile baseline (config_001, 0.485). This confirms multiprofile aggregation is a high-impact design choice. However, we have no evidence yet on whether the aggregation method itself matters. Question: config_003 is a controlled single-variable change from config_002 (avgpool → transformer), isolating the effect of aggregation architecture while holding model setting, profile setting, and RNA encoder constant. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It directly tests whether a learned transformer aggregation can outperform simple mean pooling, a question that propagates to every future multiprofile configuration (RNA-only and multimodal). (2) If transformer significantly outperforms avgpool, it redirects subsequent budget toward transformer-based multiprofile and multimodal configs; if it does not, we confirm avgpool is sufficient and can spend remaining budget exploring RNA encoders or multimodal fusion. (3) Under a tight budget, resolving this foundational aggregation question before layering in additional variables (new encoders, multimodal fusion) maximizes information per experiment."
}

